  "prediction": "The report outlines the various appointment processes for the heads of eight legislative branch agencies and entities, including the Architect of the Capitol, Comptroller General, Librarian of Congress, and others. The appointment methods range from Presidential nomination with Senate confirmation to appointment by a Congressional commission. The report also discusses past efforts to reform these appointment processes and provides information on the tenures, salaries, and most recent appointments for each agency.\n\nThe Architect of the Capitol is appointed by the President with the advice and consent of the Senate for a 10-year term. The Comptroller General is appointed by the President and confirmed by the Senate for a 15-year term. The Librarian of Congress is a Presidential appointee confirmed by the Senate, serving a 10-year term. The Government Accountability Office, Library of Congress, and other agencies have heads appointed in various ways, including by the President with Senate confirmation, by a Congressional commission, or by the Librarian of Congress.\n\nThe report also discusses past legislative efforts to alter the appointment processes for some of these positions, including the Register of Copyrights. In the 115th Congress, bills were introduced to make the Register of Copyrights a Presidential appointee confirmed by the Senate, but no action was taken on these bills before the end of the Congress. The report also provides information on the most recent appointments for each agency and their tenures.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report outlines the various appointment processes for the heads of eight legislative branch agencies and entities, including the Architect of the Capitol, Comptroller General, Librarian of Congress, and others. The appointment methods range from Presidential nomination with Senate confirmation to appointment by a Congressional commission. The report also discusses past efforts to reform these appointment processes and provides information on the tenures, salaries, and most recent appointments for each agency.\n\nThe Architect of the Capitol is appointed by the President with the advice and consent of the Senate for a 10-year term. The Comptroller General is appointed by the President and confirmed by the Senate for a 15-year term. The Librarian of Congress is a Presidential appointee confirmed by the Senate, serving a 10-year term. The Government Accountability Office, Library of Congress, and other agencies have heads appointed in various ways, including by the President with Senate confirmation, by a Congressional commission, or by the Librarian of Congress.\n\nThe report also discusses past legislative efforts to alter the appointment processes for some of these positions, including the Register of Copyrights. In the 115th Congress, bills were introduced to make the Register of Copyrights a Presidential appointee confirmed by the Senate, but no action was taken on these bills before the end of the Congress. The report also provides information on the most recent appointments for each agency and their tenures.",
  "score": 33.944953632648776,
  "answers": [
    "The leaders of the legislative branch agencies and entities\u2014the Government Accountability Office (GAO), the Library of Congress (LOC), the Congressional Research Service (CRS), the Government Publishing Office (GPO, formerly Government Printing Office), the Office of the Architect of the Capitol (AOC), the U.S. Capitol Police (USCP), the Congressional Budget Office (CBO), and the Office of Compliance\u2014are appointed in a variety of manners. Four agencies are led by a person appointed by the President, with the advice and consent of the Senate; two are appointed by Congress; one is appointed by the Librarian of Congress; and one is appointed by a board of directors. Congress has periodically examined the procedures used to appoint these officers with the aim of protecting the prerogatives of, and ensuring accountability to, Congress within the framework of the advice and consent appointment process established in Article II, Section 2 of the Constitution. This report contains information on the legislative branch agency heads' appointment processes, length of tenures (if terms are set), reappointment or removal provisions (if any), salaries and benefits, and most recent appointments. This report also briefly addresses legislation considered, but not enacted, in the 115th Congress to change the appointment process for the Register of Copyrights."
  ],
